{
  "dom_id": "step-45119",
  "name": "Customary Units of Mass",
  "problem": "Another way to measure mass is to use **customary units**. The most common customary units of mass are **ounce**, **pound**, and **ton**. \n\nA table showing some of the most common customary mass units is shown below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ton | $\\textrm{ton}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{ton} = 2,000\\,\\textrm{lb}$ |\n| Pound | $\\textrm{lb}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{lb} = 16\\,\\textrm{oz}$ |\n| Ounce | $\\textrm{oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest unit $(\\textrm{ton})$ to the smallest $(\\textrm{oz})$:\n\n- A small car could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{ton}.$\n\n- A small bag of rice has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{lb}.$\n\n- A single slice of bread could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{oz}.$\n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries.",
